  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>“Remove the cross-stack reference in stack B, so that Stack B no longer imports the exported output from Stack A.”</strong></p><p>With CloudFormation, you can safely update stack B to stop importing the value from stack A at any time. Once stack B stops importing that value, the dependency is removed and you can subsequently update or delete stack A as needed.</p><p>Let's review the other options:</p><ul><li><i>Update stack A to erase the exported output that Stack B references.</i> – This is not permitted as long as another stack is still importing the value. CloudFormation will throw an error if you attempt to remove an output that’s still being imported elsewhere.</li><li><i>Change the exported output value in Stack A, such as renaming the security group, since the import is in use.</i> – You cannot modify the exported output if another stack is referencing it. The import creates a dependency that must be removed first.</li><li><i>Delete Stack A from the AWS console because you no longer need any resources from it in this region.</i> – Deleting a stack that has exported outputs being imported by another stack (Stack B in this case) is not allowed until those imports are removed. AWS CloudFormation enforces these dependencies to avoid breaking deployments.</li></ul><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Always ensure that no other stacks are consuming exported values before making changes to exports or deleting stacks. This ensures safe and successful operations in multi-stack CloudFormation environments.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have set up two AWS CloudFormation stacks in the us-east-1 region. <br/>Stack A exports the output value of a security group. <br/>Stack B imports this value via the <code>Fn::ImportValue</code> intrinsic function in its template. <br/>Both stacks are currently in <strong>CREATE_COMPLETE</strong> status.<br/>You need to execute further operations on these stacks.<br/>Which actions listed below can you complete without issues?</p>",
